  "analysis": "Environment constraints: Python 3.12 with working SSL and network access (YouTube returned HTTP 200), but NO pip, no ensurepip, no yt-dlp/ffmpeg/whisper, and the root overlay reports 0 avail (though writes to /app actually succeed). The hint warns disk is the trap, so I must minimize downloaded media and installed packages. Plan: get yt-dlp as a standalone script (no apt needed for it), and check apt connectivity in parallel. For Zork (a text game), the 'moves' appear as typed text on screen, so OCR on video frames is the real path — but I'll first inspect the video for captions/lowest-res format to minimize bytes.",
  "plan": "1) Download yt-dlp as a standalone binary via urllib (avoids pip/apt for this tool). 2) Run apt-get update to verify Debian repos are reachable (needed for ffmpeg + tesseract-ocr later). 3) Then use yt-dlp to list the video's formats and check for any captions/subtitles that might already contain the commands. This tells me the minimal download needed before committing to any heavy pipeline.",
